From: Helengine PS3 handler <helengine-ps3@helworks.local>
Subject: [PATCH] librsx: declare a memory clobber on the context callback asm

rsxContextCallback invokes the command-buffer wrap callback through inline
assembly whose clobber list omits "memory". Because the wrapper is defined in
the same translation unit as every command emitter, GCC's interprocedural
analysis concludes the callback modifies no memory and lets callers keep
context->current / context->end cached in registers across the call. When a
command emitter triggers the wrap, it then writes its words at the stale
pre-wrap position (past the segment end, over the jump word the callback just
wrote) and stores an inconsistent current pointer. Observed with the ps3dev
gcc 13.2.0 toolchain in rsxLoadVertexProgramBlock and
rsxLoadVertexProgramParameterBlock: after "bl rsxContextCallback" execution
branches back without reloading context->current, and the RSX FIFO desyncs on
constant data a few words into the next segment.

Declaring the memory clobber makes the callback opaque to the optimizer, so
every emitter reloads the context pointers after a wrap.
---
diff --git a/ppu/include/rsx/rsx_function_macros.h b/ppu/include/rsx/rsx_function_macros.h
index bffc3a1..91ee913 100644
--- a/ppu/include/rsx/rsx_function_macros.h
+++ b/ppu/include/rsx/rsx_function_macros.h
@@ -26,7 +26,7 @@
             "addi	1,1,128\n"
             : "+r"(result)
             : "r"(context), "r"(count), "b"(context->callback)
-            : "r31", "r0", "lr"
+            : "r31", "r0", "lr", "memory"
 		);
 		return result;
 	}
